As noted by another posting, the NIC may be suspect.  Does the MX6200 NIC have a
DEC 21143 chip?  If so, Intel bought up the rights to DEC NIC chips, and rapidly
phased them out in favor of its own 8255x series.  Same remedy.  Disable the
on-board NIC and install a NIC known to be supported by XPee.

Check the Matrox web site to see which of their video cards are supported by XP.
If I recall rightly, the Millenium may not be, and the Millenium II is.  As an
alternative, consider an ATI PCI card, but not one that is too new.  If a PCI
card is too new, it will run into PCI bus compatibility issues with the main
logic board.

In any event, make the changes one hardware element at a time.  The Adaptec 7800
chip is a similar or idential to the one in the freestanding Adaptec 2940.  It
should not be the cause of the problem.  In theory, anyway.  Same drivers.

XP, even more than any other Windows release, is the cause of tons of "obsolete"
hardware, from boards to scanners to printers and on and on.  By design and with
