I think that the K-series modules were also noise immune (changed
logic thresholds).

> >
> >Is there a complete list anywhere?

There was a complete list of modules called the "option/module list". 
Two versions were produced, one was called the "edited" list contained
"current" items (basically stuff that was currently being manufactured
or repairable) and was fairly widely distributed on microfiche.  The
full list was less widely available.  It also included new items not
yet released (module numbers wre allocated fairly early in the design
process) and obsolete stuff.

> Unless someone has compiled one, no. DEC pspecial-purpose cards were
> documented in the machines they were part of, the general-purpose run
> in the annual Logic Handbooks.
> 
> The only color unmentioned was grey - a simple handle supplied to
> those rolling their own meaning (nothing DEC but the handle)
> 
> The numbers on the R/B/M s did generally tell general function
> 
> where C is color and n is number
> 
> C-0nn - simple arrays of diodes
> C-1nn - simple gates, NOTS and the like
> C-2nn - flip-flops and slices of CPUs
> C-3nn - delay lines and the like
> C-4nn - clocks
> C-5nn/6nn etc - special complex functions

> >Do later cards such as VAX L or T cards have a "color"?  I know many
> >M series cards had metal handles but they still stamped the part # on
> >them and inked the stamped numbers magenta.
> 
> No, the color system was dead and they just needed codes for 'em

The initial letter changed from indicating the type of
electronics/function of the module to the mechanical design of the
module.  I believe that the first time this was done was the Lxxxx
modules for the VAX 11/750 which were (I think) the same size as the
"superhex" M-series modules used in the 11/780 but used a quite
different type of backplane connector.  The number changed from
classifying a module by function to just being a unique identifier
occurred with the M-series when increasing levels of logic integration
meant that modules changed from being logic building blocks (the
business DEC had been founded on) to being complete functional units. 
The numbers were not totally rendomly allocated though.  The
engineering group I worked was allocated M31xx for most of the stuff
we designed (I think that's correct - I'm trying to remember things
from getting on 20 years ago here).

Some letters were also reused.  For example, S followed by a 4-digit
number (I think these were S-box modules) had nothing to do with the
old Flip-Chip? S-series modules (many were repackaged M-series
modules).  IIRC apart from the original system modules 4-digit numbers
were never used before M-series modules appeared.

