A correction:
On Thu, 4 Nov 2004 04:35:28 +0000 (UTC),
moroney@world.std.spaamtrap.com (Michael Moroney) wrote:

This is the whole list of colors:

The first DEC System Building Blocks were 2x the size of flipchips,
and deep, because of their aluminum frame and big Amphenol connector
at the end - they came in speeds rated by the first of the four
numbers engraved on the side opposite the plug, 1000 and 4000 at 5
"Megacycles"<remember megacycles?> according to Digital Modules (pub
A-705, announcing , in the back, the PDP-1) The 6000- series ran at
10. The modules were also available in even larger aluminum boxes,
with the connector run out to the front, with a logic diagram and
patch cord sockets, so you could string them together and build
something without soldering a backplane.

One thing allowing the FlipChip was the automated Gardner-Denver wire
wrap machine, eliminating the hours of soldering that went into the
PDP-1,4,5,6 and older sections of the PDP-7.

>Very old DEC "Flip Chip" cards as well as many more modern cards for
>PDP8s and PDP11s were coded with a color as well as a letter (part of the
>card part #) as to the type of logic or type of card.  Ones I know are:
>
>M Magenta        TTL IC logic and gate arrays (VERY numerous in PDP11s)
Later PDP-11 boards that lacked FlipChip handles still carried the
designation Mnnnn

>R Red            RTL logic
>S Red (scarlet?)No - Faster RTL- No
                Special - not Faster, but a larger fan-out for certain
circuits in the PDP-8 (Straight-8)An S card can replace an R card with
the same number, but not necesserally the reverse.
>A Amber          Analog cards & D/A A/D converters
>W White          Do-it-yourself or interface cards, 
IO buses and lamp controllers
>G Green          Memory and Power Supply
>B Blue            Fast DTL - used extensively in the 7-9-KA10 
My favorite was the R-210 flop - the PDP-9 and much of the KA-10 were
built out of these brute-force speedsters, released with 2W carbon
pullup resistors - which caught fire. ECO'ed to a pair of 5W carbon
pullups
>K blacK          Some sort of controller interface.
Actually, an M board with more isolation and boosted power control
capability for building industrial control units, either special,
PDP-14 or PDP-16 controllers
<grey> - sold to folks rolling their own start to finish, grey handles
were meant to signify "nothing by dec except this handle here"
>
>Is there a complete list anywhere?

Depends - cards particular to machines (like the B-210 were mentioned
in the machine's manuals. General purpose cards were mentioned in the
annual Logic Handbooks. Someone may have put together a comprehensive
listing:

In general, for B,M,R,S cards: Where C stands for color (through the
PDP-15)
C-0nn - simple diode arrays
C-1nn - simple gates, like Nots
C-2nn - flipflops - also machine-specific CPU slices
C-3nn - time delays, etc.
C-4nn - clocks
C-5nn/6nn - assorted complex circuits.

Anything with a number greater than 1000 was not really a single
circuit, but a large chunk of a machine.
>Do later cards such as VAX L or T cards have a "color"?  I know many
>M series cards had metal handles but they still stamped the part # on
>them and inked the stamped numbers magenta.

See above Ls and Ts are colorless, the system went the way of the dodo
(as far as DEC was concerned) with the metal-handled lock-down module.

>I vaguely remember seeing a core memory card with a green handle and a H 
>part number (usually power supplies or cabinets).  Is there such a thing
>as a H color?  Did R and S cards use the same color?

H is not a color, simply a designation for Hardware and Power 


